<section class="reference-section" id="krishna-kaliya"><div class="reference-section-heading"><h2>Krishna and Kaliya</h2><a href="/krishna-kaliya.html">Return to story</a></div><div class="reference-content">
<p><strong>Srimad Bhagavata Purana · Skandha 10 · chapters 16–17.</strong> This original condensed English retelling follows the verse sequence in the Bhaktivedanta Book Trust’s VedaBase edition. That is a Vaishnava devotional translation with commentary; the retelling uses the narrative verses and chapter sequence, while keeping the edition’s interpretive commentary distinct.</p>
<div class="source-list"><a href="https://vedabase.io/en/library/sb/10/16/" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><span>Bhagavata 10.16 · Krishna chastises Kaliya</span><small>VedaBase · Sanskrit, translation and Vaishnava commentary</small></a><a href="https://vedabase.io/en/library/sb/10/17/" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><span>Bhagavata 10.17 · Kaliya’s history</span><small>VedaBase · Sanskrit, translation and Vaishnava commentary</small></a></div>
<ol><li><strong>10.16.4–8</strong><small>The poisoned lake, Krishna’s decision to purify it and his leap from the kadamba tree.</small></li><li><strong>10.16.9–23</strong><small>Kaliya’s attack, the distress of the cowherds and the people of Vraja, and Krishna’s release from the coils.</small></li><li><strong>10.16.24–30</strong><small>Krishna circles Kaliya and dances upon the raised hoods.</small></li><li><strong>10.16.31–53</strong><small>The Nagapatnis approach with their children and pray for Kaliya’s life.</small></li><li><strong>10.16.54–59</strong><small>Kaliya submits and Krishna releases him.</small></li><li><strong>10.16.60–67</strong><small>The command to depart, protection from Garuda and the restoration of the Yamuna.</small></li><li><strong>10.17.1–12</strong><small>Kaliya’s conflict with Garuda and the reason he had taken refuge in the Yamuna lake.</small></li><li><strong>10.17.13–20</strong><small>Krishna emerges and is reunited with Yashoda, Nanda, Balarama and Vraja.</small></li></ol>
<p>The page summarizes rather than translates the Nagapatnis’ long theological prayer. The non-graphic artwork condenses the text’s many serpent hoods for visual clarity; imagined clothing, landscape and figures are not historical reconstructions. The pause-and-reflect question is editorial.</p>
